# Per-developer settings for the agent VM.
#
# Copy this file to `vagrant/local.env` and customize. That copy
# is gitignored: it holds values that differ per person, so
# committing one person's would give everyone else's VM the wrong
# identity.
#
# It IS pushed to the VM host along with the rest of `vagrant/`,
# which is how it reaches the guest. Keep secrets out of it -- the
# optional deploy key deliberately lives on the VM host instead,
# see the Vagrantfile.
#
# Format: key=value, no quotes, `#` for comments.

# Identity for commits the agent makes inside the VM.
#
# Without these, provisioning still succeeds but sets no git
# identity, and any commit made in the VM fails with "Please tell
# me who you are".
#
# Marking the VM in the name is worth doing even when the email
# matches your own: `git log` then shows at a glance which commits
# came from an agent rather than from you.
#
# GitHub's noreply address (Settings > Emails, with "Keep my email
# addresses private") keeps your real address out of published
# history while still attributing commits to you.
GIT_USER_NAME=Your Name (agent VM)
GIT_USER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Timezone for the guest, e.g. `Europe/Ljubljana` or `Etc/UTC`.
#
# Leave it out and the VM keeps the box's own default, which is
# `Etc/UTC` on the Debian cloud image. That is fine for comparing a
# VM's timestamps against CI, and mildly annoying for comparing
# them against your own clock -- which is why this is a per-person
# setting rather than a value committed for everyone.
#
# The name must be one `timedatectl list-timezones` prints.
# Provisioning fails on anything else rather than warning and
# leaving the clock as it was.
# TIMEZONE=Europe/Ljubljana
